An abrupt shift towards a hotter and drier climate regime in the Western Eurasian Steppe
                        Jinling Piao 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-8775-15761,2, Jong-Seong Kug 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0003-2251-25792, Wen Chen 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-9327-90793,4, Mi-Kyung Sung 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-7659-65715, Shangfeng Chen 
            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0003-4347-85921, Zhikai Wang1, Qingyu Cai3,4 & …Danbi Lee2 Show authors

Nature Geoscience
                             (2026) Cite this article









            Save article




                View saved research

AbstractSemiarid drylands, with their strong land–atmosphere coupling, are increasingly recognized as highly sensitive to anthropogenic forcing. Yet whether this sensitivity can give rise to abrupt hydroclimatic transitions under sustained warming remains unclear. Here, using reanalysis data and large-ensemble and multi-model climate simulations, we identify an abrupt climate regime shift in the Western Eurasian Steppe, marked by unprecedented summer drying that exceeds the range of natural variability over the past century. This drying transition shows no clear sign of recovery during the observational period (1901–2024). External forcing provided the background drying, while internal climate variability amplified the regional trend since the early 1990s. Declining soil moisture weakens evaporative cooling and strengthens sensible heat flux, intensifying heat extremes and deepening hydrological deficits. This intensified land–atmosphere feedback has amplified drying and heat extremes to unprecedented levels over the past six decades, contributing to widespread land degradation and reduced gross primary productivity. The Western Eurasian Steppe thus serves as an early warning signal for other Northern Hemisphere mid-latitude drylands, where similar land–atmosphere feedbacks may also promote abrupt and persistent regime shifts under continued warming.

