          Additional informationPublisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.Extended dataExtended Data Fig. 1 Change in summer SPEI over the Western Eurasian Steppe.a,c,e, 11-year running mean of summer SPEI values before and after the turning point (TP), based on different combinations of PRE and PET datasets: CRU PRE and PET (a), PRECL PRE and TERRA PET (c), and PRECL PRE and CRU PET (e). Blue and orange dots represent the running mean before and after the TP, with blue and red lines corresponding to the linear fits for these periods. The numbers indicate the rate of change in SPEI (yr⁻1), and an asterisk (*) marks significant changes at the P ≤ 0.05 level. b,d,f, The corresponding 7-year running trends of the summer SPEI values as shown in (a,c,e), with the x-axis representing the central year of each 7-year window. Blue and red bars indicate positive and negative trends, respectively. The vertical dashed gray line marks the period of the strongest drying tendency during 2004–2010.Extended Data Fig. 2 Regime shift in summer SPEI in the Western Eurasian Steppe.a–c. Time series of summer SPEI over the Western Eurasian Steppe (40°–55°N, 30°–65°E) based on different combinations of PRE and PET datasets: CRU PRE and PET (a), PRECL PRE and TERRA PET (b), and PRECL PRE and CRU PET (c). The black vertical lines mark the year 2006, when a significant regime shift is detected using the STARS method, surpassing the 95% confidence level. The blue and red lines represent the mean SPEI before and after 2006, respectively.Extended Data Fig. 3 Changes in summer surface air temperature (SAT) and precipitation (PRE) over the Western Eurasian Steppe.a,b, Linear trends of summer SAT (a) and PRE (b) derived from the CRU dataset during 1991–2020. Hatching indicates trends significant at the 95% confidence level based on the MK test. c,d, Time series of summer SAT (c) and PRE (d) (relative to the 1981–2010 mean) over the Western Eurasian Steppe. The black line in (c) indicates summer SAT anomalies averaged over the terrestrial Northern Hemisphere. All series are smoothed using an 11-year running mean.Extended Data Fig. 4 Externally and internally driven drying trend.a,c,e, Trend in summer SPEI during 1992–2010 based on the ensemble mean (EM) of MPI-GE (n = 100) (a), MIROC6 (n = 50) (c) and CMIP6 (n = 18) (e). Hatching indicates that more than two thirds of model ensembles share the same-sign changes. b, Signal-to-noise ratio of trends in summer SPEI derived from MPI-GE ensembles. Values larger-than-1 are hatched. d,f, Differences in trends of summer SPEI during 1992–2010 between MIN10 and MAX10 for MIROC6 (d), and between MIN9 and MAX9 for CMIP6 (f). Hatching indicates differences significant at the 95% confidence level based on the Student’s t test.Extended Data Fig. 5 Summer SPEI trends in AMIP simulations.a,b, Linear trends of summer (June–August) SPEI based on the ensemble mean of the AMIP experiment for MPI-ESM1-2-LR (r1i1p1f1, r2i1p1f1, r3i1p1f1) (a) and MIROC6 (r1i1p1f1 through r10i1p1f1) (b), excluding regions where the aridity index (the ratio of annual precipitation (PRE) to potential evapotranspiration (PET)) is less than 0.05. Hatching indicates trends significant at the 95% confidence level based on the Mann-Kendall (MK) test.Extended Data Fig. 6 Changes in land-atmosphere feedback and its impact on land cover types.a,b, Trends in summer surface air temperature anomalies (T’, σ) (a), and anomalies in the contribution of soil moisture deficit to the surface sensible heat flux (e’, σ) (b) during 1992–2010 (per decade). Hatching indicates trends significant at the 95% confidence level based on MK test. c, Time series showing changes in the areal fraction of grassland (blue line) and barren areas (red line) within the Western Eurasian Steppe relative to their pre-2010 mean. d, Changes in the areal fraction of grassland (blue bars) and barren areas (red bars) during different periods relative to their pre-2010 mean.Extended Data Fig. 7 Changes in summer land-atmosphere coupling strength over the Western Eurasian Steppe.a–d. Time series of summer soil moisture anomalies over the Western Eurasian Steppe (40°–55°N, 30°–65°E) obtained from ERA5 (a), GLEAM (b), ESACCI (c), and land–atmosphere coupling strength (π, σ) (d). The black vertical lines mark the year when a significant regime shift is detected using the STARS method, surpassing the 95% confidence level. The blue and red lines represent the mean soil moisture and π values before and after the regime shift, respectively.Extended Data Fig. 8 Role of soil moisture–atmosphere (SA) coupling in the emergence of the simulated hydroclimatic regime shift.Summer SPEI time series from seven CMIP6 models participating in LS3MIP. a, fully coupled historical and SSP5-8.5 simulations (Hist&SSP5-8.5); b, corresponding LFMIP-pdLC simulations, in which soil moisture is prescribed to its climatological seasonal cycle while external forcing remains identical. Red vertical lines denote statistically significant regime shifts detected using the STARS method. Five of the seven fully coupled simulations exhibit significant post-1990 regime shifts (1994–2013), whereas none of the corresponding LFMIP-pdLC simulations exhibits a significant post-1990 regime shift after interactive soil moisture–atmosphere coupling is suppressed.Extended Data Fig. 9 Simulated Changes in summer drying conditions over the Western Eurasian Steppe with and without anthropogenic forcing.Bars show the 19-year running average of summer SPEI derived from piControl simulations of 14 CMIP6 models, representing pre-industrial natural variability with no human-induced forcing included (green). Grey shading denotes the 5th–95th percentile range of natural variability. Vertical lines indicate summer SPEI during the post-regime period 2006–2024 based on the observational mean (red solid line), individual model means (dashed lines) and multi-model mean (blue solid line) of the Hist&SSP2-4.5 simulations with human-induced forcing included. All SPEI series are smoothed using an 11-year running mean.Extended Data Fig. 10 Multi-model projections of summer drying in the Western Eurasian Steppe.a, Changes in summer SPEI relative to the 1981–2010 baseline for 2021–2040, 2041–2060, and 2061–2080 under different future scenarios in MPI-GE (n = 100) (a), MIROC6 (n = 50) (b) and CMIP6 (n = 18) (c). Thin and thick red lines represent the ensemble range and ensemble mean, respectively.Supplementary informationSupplementary Information (download PDF )Supplementary Methods 1 and 2, Figs. 1–13 and Table 1.Peer Review File (download PDF )Rights and permissionsSpringer Nature or its licensor (e.g. a society or other partner) holds exclusive rights to this article under a publishing agreement with the author(s) or other rightsholder(s); author self-archiving of the accepted manuscript version of this article is solely governed by the terms of such publishing agreement and applicable law.Reprints and permissionsAbout this articleCite this articlePiao, J., Kug, JS., Chen, W. et al. An abrupt shift towards a hotter and drier climate regime in the Western Eurasian Steppe.
